{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyeong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jinyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhyeok","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Junhyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yumin","familyName":"Shim","name":"Shim, Yumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joohyeok","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Joohyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunyoung","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Sunyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seong Jae","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Seong Jae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interpreting vision transformers via residual replacement model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T07:00:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:13:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-22T11:28:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-23T00:54:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"How do vision transformers (ViTs) represent and process the world? This paper addresses this long-standing question through the first systematic analysis of 6.6K features across all layers, extracted via sparse autoencoders, and by introducing the residual replacement model, which replaces ViT computations with interpretable features in the residual stream. Our analysis reveals not only a feature evolution from low-level patterns to high-level semantics, but also how ViTs encode curves and spatial positions through specialized feature types. The residual replacement model scalably produces a faithful yet parsimonious circuit for human-scale interpretability by significantly simplifying the original computations. As a result, this framework enables intuitive understanding of ViT mechanisms. Finally, we demonstrate the utility of our framework in debiasing spurious correlations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:11:07Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:11:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-23T02:50:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16444","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16444","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16444","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhan","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Chenhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir M.","familyName":"Rahmani","name":"Rahmani, Amir M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Domain-Specific Constitutional AI: Enhancing Safety in LLM-Powered Mental Health Chatbots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; J.3; K.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-19T21:46:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:14:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T03:08:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-23T01:13:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/bsn66969.2025.11337405","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mental health applications have emerged as a critical area in computational health, driven by rising global rates of mental illness, the integration of AI in psychological care, and the need for scalable solutions in underserved communities. These include therapy chatbots, crisis detection, and wellness platforms handling sensitive data, requiring specialized AI safety beyond general safeguards due to emotional vulnerability, risks like misdiagnosis or symptom exacerbation, and precise management of vulnerable states to avoid severe outcomes such as self-harm or loss of trust. Despite AI safety advances, general safeguards inadequately address mental health-specific challenges, including crisis intervention accuracy to avert escalations, therapeutic guideline adherence to prevent misinformation, scale limitations in resource-constrained settings, and adaptation to nuanced dialogues where generics may introduce biases or miss distress signals. We introduce an approach to apply Constitutional AI training with domain-specific mental health principles for safe, domain-adapted CAI systems in computational mental health applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to 2025 IEEE 21st International Conference on Body Sensor Networks (BSN)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16444","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:48:32Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:48:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-23T02:50:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingzhen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yingzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiheng","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Yiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aijing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Aijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georges El","familyName":"Fakhri","name":"Fakhri, Georges El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SAM-DCE: Decoupled Context Enhancement for Prompt-Free Medical Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T02:39:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:43:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T09:14:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:43:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T04:16:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-22T00:34:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Segment Anything Model (SAM) is a promising foundation model for medical image segmentation, but its prompt-driven design is not directly suitable for fully automatic multi-class anatomical segmentation. Without manual point, box, or mask prompts, SAM faces a dual over-smoothing problem: deep image features may lose fine local structural evidence, while mask tokens may become semantically ambiguous. We propose SAM-DCE, a decoupled context enhancement framework for prompt-free medical image segmentation. SAM-DCE introduces Spatial Representation Compensation (SRC) to recover anatomical evidence from intermediate ViT features and Semantic Token Calibration (STC) to improve the category consistency of foreground mask tokens. Rather than replacing the pretrained decoder or fusing output masks, SAM-DCE refines the spatial and semantic representations before recombining them through SAM's original token-to-mask mechanism. On the Synapse multi-organ CT segmentation dataset, SAM-DCE achieved a mean Dice score of 83.86\\% and an HD95 of 16.04 mm, outperforming the representative CNN-, Transformer-, and SAM-based baselines evaluated here. Ablation experiments further showed that SRC and STC provide complementary improvements, supporting the proposed spatial--semantic factorization for prompt-free SAM adaptation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:58:54Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:58:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-22T03:00:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16998","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16998","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16998","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishka","familyName":"Khendry","name":"Khendry, Nishka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Margadji","name":"Margadji, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian W.","familyName":"Pattinson","name":"Pattinson, Sebastian W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IDfRA: Self-Verification for Iterative Design in Robotic Assembly"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T09:31:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:50:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T20:29:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-16T00:14:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/icra57385.2026.11697014","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Design for Robotic Assembly (DfRA) remains largely dependent on manual planning and heuristic simulation, limiting scalability and robustness in complex industrial settings. Although large language models (LLMs) show promise for semantic reasoning and task planning, most approaches remain tightly coupled to pre-built simulators that assume an accurate world model. We introduce Iterative Design for Robotic Assembly (IDfRA), a closed-loop framework that combines an LLM for plan generation with a vision-language model (VLM) for execution assessment. Given a target structure and a partial environmental signature, the LLM proposes an assembly plan, the robot executes it once at test time, and the VLM evaluates the resulting state to provide feedback for replanning. Through this iterative planning-execution-verification loop, the system progressively improves semantic fidelity and physical feasibility. Crucially, IDfRA does not require an accurate a priori world model before deployment. Instead, physical constraints are discovered online through interaction, enabling adaptation to under-specified environments. Empirical evaluation demonstrates that IDfRA attains 73.3% top-1 accuracy in semantic recognisability, surpassing the baseline on this metric. Moreover, the resulting assembly plans exhibit robust physical feasibility, achieving an overall 86.9% construction success rate, with design quality improving across iterations, albeit not always monotonically. Pairwise human evaluation further corroborates the advantages of IDfRA relative to alternative approaches. By integrating self-verification with context-aware adaptation, the framework evidences strong potential for deployment in unstructured manufacturing scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 8 figures. Published at ICRA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16998","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:01:35Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:01:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:15:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16615","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16615","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16615","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jelle","familyName":"Luijkx","name":"Luijkx, Jelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyu","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Runyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zlatan","familyName":"Ajanović","name":"Ajanović, Zlatan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jens","familyName":"Kober","name":"Kober, Jens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Guided Task- and Affordance-Level Exploration in Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.9; I.2.6; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T40","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T10:37:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:26:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-14T09:38:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T01:15:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/icra57385.2026.11695912","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) is a promising approach for robotic manipulation, but it can suffer from low sample efficiency and requires extensive exploration of large state-action spaces. Recent methods leverage the commonsense knowledge and reasoning abilities of large language models (LLMs) to guide exploration toward more meaningful states. However, LLMs can produce plans that are semantically plausible yet physically infeasible, yielding unreliable behavior. We introduce LLM-TALE, a framework that uses LLMs' planning to directly steer RL exploration. LLM-TALE integrates planning at both the task level and the affordance level, improving learning efficiency by directing agents toward semantically meaningful actions. Unlike prior approaches that assume optimal LLM-generated plans or rewards, LLM-TALE corrects suboptimality online and explores multimodal affordance-level plans without human supervision. We evaluate LLM-TALE on pick-and-place tasks in standard RL benchmarks, observing improvements in both sample efficiency and success rates over strong baselines. Real-robot experiments indicate promising zero-shot sim-to-real transfer. Code and supplementary material are available at llm-tale.github.io."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures, ICRA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16615","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:33Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:15:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18088","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18088","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18088","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuhao","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Chuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evangelos","familyName":"Pournaras","name":"Pournaras, Evangelos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimization without Future Compromises? Decentralized Coordination via Collective and Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:58:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:50:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-23T14:40:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-24T01:08:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient resource allocation in multi-agent systems requires autonomous agents to coordinate their decisions while balancing system-wide objectives with individual costs. This becomes increasingly challenging over long time horizons, where decisions that improve the current allocation may compromise future resource allocation, while decentralized agents have limited observations of the overall system. Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) can learn such long-term dependencies via local observations, but directly applying it to large-scale coordination leads to rapidly growing decision spaces and inefficient training. To this end, we propose Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning (HRCL), a hierarchical framework that uses MARL to guide, rather than replace, decentralized multi-agent coordination. At the high level, MARL learns strategies that restrict the alternatives considered during coordination and guide agents in balancing system-wide and individual objectives. At the low level, agents perform efficient decentralized coordination under this strategic guidance. This separation reduces the learning space and allows short-term coordination trade-offs to be evaluated according to their long-term effects. Experiments on a synthetic benchmark show that HRCL converges substantially faster than standalone MARL and reduces system-wide and individual costs by 35.53% and 27.05%, respectively. Evaluations on energy self-management and drone swarm sensing further show improved resource allocation, power-peak regulation, and sensing efficiency. These results show that learning strategic guidance for an existing coordination process can retain scalable decentralized coordination without letting short-term decisions compromise future resource allocation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18088","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:27:18Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:27:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-24T02:49:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Gómez-Torres","name":"Gómez-Torres, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manel","familyName":"Molina-Ruiz","name":"Molina-Ruiz, Manel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Privitera","name":"Privitera, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enric","familyName":"Menéndez","name":"Menéndez, Enric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Llibertat","familyName":"Abad","name":"Abad, Llibertat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordi","familyName":"Sort","name":"Sort, Jordi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Bourgeois","name":"Bourgeois, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier","familyName":"Rodriguez-Viejo","name":"Rodriguez-Viejo, Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aitor","familyName":"Lopeandia","name":"Lopeandia, Aitor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Microsecond-Pulsed Nanocalorimetry: A Scalable Approach for Ultrasensitive Heat Capacity Measurements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:58:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:48:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T20:05:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T01:50:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1038/s41378-026-01426-7","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce a nanocalorimetric technique based on microsecond-pulsed heating (μs-PHnC) that enables high-sensitivity, quasi-isothermal heat capacity measurements on nanoscale samples. Such resolution is critical for exploring thermodynamic signatures in low-dimensional materials, where conventional techniques fall short. By confining thermal excitation to microsecond timescales, this approach minimizes lateral heat diffusion, reduces heat capacity addenda to below 10^{-9} J K^{-1}, and achieves noise densities as low as 75 pJ K^{-1} Hz^{-1/2} mm^{-2}, unlocking precise thermodynamic characterization of subnanogram samples in areas as small as 30 x 30 μm^{2}. The method delivers exceptional temperature homogeneity, as demonstrated by resolving sharp phase transitions, such as the antiferromagnetic transition in ultrathin CoO films, with unprecedented clarity. Its quasi-static operation is inherently compatible with external stimuli, including magnetic and electric fields, thereby expanding its utility for in-operando thermodynamic studies. This advancement establishes a robust and scalable platform for probing thermal phenomena in nanostructured and low-dimensional materials, significantly broadening the scope of nanocalorimetry."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:25:42Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:25:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:43:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17411","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17411","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17411","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Siqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Molei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Molei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziye","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Ziye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuan","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Mixture Models for Algorithmic Fairness Under Latent Heterogeneity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T07:03:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:14:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-18T05:09:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-21T00:26:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Machine learning models optimized for average performance can perform poorly on vulnerable subpopulations. Existing approaches often rely on groups specified in advance, yet fairness-relevant subgroup structure may be latent, intersectional, and driven by complex interactions among continuous and discrete attributes. We introduce \\textbf{ROME} (\\textbf{\\underline{RO}}bust \\textbf{\\underline{M}}ixture \\textbf{\\underline{E}}nsemble), a framework that learns latent group structure while optimizing worst-group predictive performance. ROME connects latent-variable modeling with distributionally robust optimization (DRO) through two complementary approaches: an Expectation-Maximization formulation with robust aggregation for linear models and a neural Mixture-of-Experts formulation for nonlinear settings. Across simulations and three real-world regression datasets, ROME improves worst-group performance while maintaining competitive overall accuracy, including in comparisons with established group-aware and group-label-free robust learning methods. ROME provides a flexible approach to robust prediction when fairness-relevant attributes are available for subgroup discovery but their direct use in group-specific outcome models is restricted."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17411","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:11:22Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:11:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-21T02:23:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16493","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16493","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16493","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyoung Hun","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Kyoung Hun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglie","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Honglie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B. Q.","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, B. Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfred","familyName":"Zong","name":"Zong, Alfred","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiaomei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qiaomei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyeongbo","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Gyeongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeongi","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Hyeongi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyun-Woo J.","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyun-Woo J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyeok","familyName":"Ha","name":"Ha, Seunghyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehwon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jaehwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suchismita","familyName":"Sarker","name":"Sarker, Suchismita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob P. C.","familyName":"Ruff","name":"Ruff, Jacob P. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaozhe","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Xiaozhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Duan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephen","familyName":"Weathersby","name":"Weathersby, Stephen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Kramer","name":"Kramer, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxin","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongsung","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Dongsung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doron","familyName":"Azoury","name":"Azoury, Doron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masataka","familyName":"Mogi","name":"Mogi, Masataka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B. J.","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, B. J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"N. L.","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, N. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoyoung","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Hoyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuh","familyName":"Gedik","name":"Gedik, Nuh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamics of a jointly commensurate moiré charge density wave"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T01:52:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:13:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-01T18:57:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:06:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-03T01:37:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-18T00:08:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1038/s41467-026-77670-2","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advent of two-dimensional moiré systems has revolutionized the exploration of phenomena arising from strong correlations and nontrivial band topology. Recently, a moiré superstructure formed by two coexisting charge density waves (CDWs) with slightly mismatched wavevectors has been realized. These incommensurate CDWs can collectively exhibit commensurability, resulting in the jointly commensurate CDW (JC-CDW) and establishing a new paradigm for controlling moiré potential and periodicity. Achieving such functionality, however, hinges on a key open question: how do the amplitude, phase coherence, and periodicity of this order respond to external perturbations? Here, we address this question using a suite of time- and momentum-resolved diffraction and spectroscopic techniques to probe light-induced CDW dynamics in EuTe$_4$. Our time-resolved diffraction measurements distinguish the instantaneous quenching of the JC-CDW amplitude, as verified by time-resolved photoemission spectroscopy, from the much slower evolution of phase fluctuations. Furthermore, while the JC-CDW wavevector remains locked along the CDW direction upon photoexcitation, indicating a preserved moiré periodicity, the correlation length of JC-CDW shows an exclusive reduction perpendicular to its wavevector, unveiling the formation of previously unexplored shear-type defects. Together, this multimodal methodology reconstructs the spatiotemporal evolution of the JC-CDW upon excitation. These findings not only highlight the remarkable robustness of JC-CDWs out of equilibrium, but also provide insight into optical manipulation and engineering of moiré quantum materials through defect control."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16493","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:49:42Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:49:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:44:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiaxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jen-tse","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jen-tse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Man Ho","familyName":"Lam","name":"Lam, Man Ho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xintao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xintao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"Sap","name":"Sap, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The PIMMUR Principles: Ensuring Validity in Collective Behavior of LLM Societies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:27:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:49:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T14:24:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:31:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T17:12:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:40:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-10T01:02:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-11T00:17:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used to simulate human collective behavior, yet claims that such simulations are human-like remain largely untested. We conducted a systematic audit (pre-registered on OSF) of LLM-based social simulations across four databases (Scopus, IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, and arXiv). Across 576 studies reported in 350 recent papers, we applied six methodological evaluations: agent Profile, Interaction, Memory, Minimal-Control, Unawareness, and Realism (PIMMUR). Coding every study against pre-specified rules, we revealed that PIM were met more often than MUR. Frontier LLMs correctly identified the underlying social experiment in 65.2% of cases, and 50.6% of prompts imposed constraints that pre-determined the outcome. These compliance rates are upper bounds, because incomplete methodological reporting (for example, unreleased prompts) limits the available evidence. Reproducing five representative experiments (e.g., opinion dynamics), we found that reported collective phenomena often vanish or reverse once PIMMUR principles are enforced, indicating that many \"emergent\" behaviors are methodological artifacts rather than genuine social dynamics. Current LLM simulations may therefore capture model-specific biases rather than universal features of human social behavior, raising concerns about their use as scientific proxies for human society."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Added more studies in our systematic audit (350 papers; 576 simulations)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:26:29Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:26:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-11T02:36:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17920","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17920","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17920","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jamiyan","familyName":"Sukhbaatar","name":"Sukhbaatar, Jamiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satoshi","familyName":"Imamura","name":"Imamura, Satoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibuki","familyName":"Inoue","name":"Inoue, Ibuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoya","familyName":"Murakami","name":"Murakami, Shoya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazi Mahmudul","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Kazi Mahmudul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungwoo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Seungwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ingon","familyName":"Chanpornpakdi","name":"Chanpornpakdi, Ingon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshihisa","familyName":"Tanaka","name":"Tanaka, Toshihisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SingLEM: Single-Channel Large EEG Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T15:46:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:43:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-03T04:32:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T01:28:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-07T10:25:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:36:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/jbhi.2026.3732223","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current deep learning models for electroencephalography (EEG) are often task-specific and depend on large labeled datasets, limiting their adaptability. Although EEG foundation models seek broader applicability, many still rely on predefined multi-channel inputs, electrode-layout assumptions, or model-specific channel handling. To address these limitations, we introduce the Single-Channel Large EEG Model (SingLEM), a self-supervised foundation model whose hybrid convolutional--Transformer encoder maps each channel independently to a reusable representation capturing local and long-range temporal structure. These representations can be used individually or combined through channel-wise feature concatenation. We assembled 71 public EEG datasets comprising approximately 9,200 subjects and 357,000 single-channel hours. For leakage-controlled evaluation, downstream results were obtained with a model pretrained on 68 datasets after excluding the three source datasets underlying the six tasks. A model pretrained on all 71 datasets is provided for general reuse. Across six motor imagery and cognitive tasks under strict leave-one-subject-out (LOSO) evaluation, the leakage-controlled model with concatenated representations and a support vector machine (SVM) classifier achieved the best overall performance among the compared pretrained and classical feature-based methods. Additional classifier and subject-adapted analyses supported the robustness of its representations. These findings support single-channel self-supervised learning as a montage-flexible foundation for reusable EEG feature extraction and electrode-level spatial analysis. The source code and pretrained models are available at https://github.com/ttlabtuat/SingLEM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in IEEE Journal of Biomedical and Health Informatics. Updated to the accepted manuscript following peer review, including revised experiments, analyses, and figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17920","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:23:23Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:23:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:54:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Amir","familyName":"Kasaei","name":"Kasaei, Seyed Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Aghayari","name":"Aghayari, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Marioriyad","name":"Marioriyad, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niki","familyName":"Sepasian","name":"Sepasian, Niki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shayan Baghayi","familyName":"Nejad","name":"Nejad, Shayan Baghayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"MohammadAmin","familyName":"Fazli","name":"Fazli, MohammadAmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdieh Soleymani","familyName":"Baghshah","name":"Baghshah, Mahdieh Soleymani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Hossein","familyName":"Rohban","name":"Rohban, Mohammad Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CARINOX: Inference-time Scaling with Category-Aware Reward-based Initial Noise Optimization and Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T07:51:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:33:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T15:47:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T02:01:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-11T12:35:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:35:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-05T14:37:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-09T00:37:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image diffusion models, such as Stable Diffusion, can produce high-quality and diverse images but often fail to achieve compositional alignment, particularly when prompts describe complex object relationships, attributes, or spatial arrangements. Recent inference-time approaches address this by optimizing or exploring the initial noise under the guidance of reward functions that score text-image alignment without requiring model fine-tuning. While promising, each strategy has intrinsic limitations when used alone: optimization can stall due to poor initialization or unfavorable search trajectories, whereas exploration may require a prohibitively large number of samples to locate a satisfactory output. Our analysis further shows that neither single reward metrics nor ad-hoc combinations reliably capture all aspects of compositionality, leading to weak or inconsistent guidance. To overcome these challenges, we present Category-Aware Reward-based Initial Noise Optimization and EXploration (CARINOX), a unified framework that combines noise optimization and exploration with a principled reward selection procedure grounded in correlation with human judgments. Evaluations on two complementary benchmarks covering diverse compositional challenges show that CARINOX raises average alignment scores by approximately 19% on both T2I-CompBench++ and HRS, consistently outperforming state-of-the-art optimization and exploration-based methods across all major categories, while preserving image quality and diversity. The project page is available at https://amirkasaei.com/carinox/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to TMLR (2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:12:28Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:12:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:54:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yajing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yajing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min-Yen","familyName":"Kan","name":"Kan, Min-Yen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KAHAN: Knowledge-Augmented Hierarchical Analysis and Narration for Financial Data Narration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T11:15:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-09T02:44:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1405","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose KAHAN, a knowledge-augmented hierarchical framework that systematically extracts insights from raw tabular data at entity, pairwise, group, and system levels. KAHAN uniquely leverages LLMs as domain experts to drive the analysis. On DataTales financial reporting benchmark, KAHAN outperforms existing approaches by over 20% on narrative quality (GPT-4o), maintains 98.2% factuality, and demonstrates practical utility in human evaluation. Our results reveal that knowledge quality drives model performance through distillation, hierarchical analysis benefits vary with market complexity, and the framework transfers effectively to healthcare domains. The data and code are available at https://github.com/yajingyang/kahan."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:02:30Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:02:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:53:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16691","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16691","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16691","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binglei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Binglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dejia","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Dejia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaxia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Huaxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nemo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Nemo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InstanceAssemble: Layout-Aware Image Generation via Instance Assembling Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T13:37:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:31:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T03:37:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:43:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.52202/085713-3849","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in generating high-quality images. Recent advancements in Layout-to-Image (L2I) generation have leveraged positional conditions and textual descriptions to facilitate precise and controllable image synthesis. Despite overall progress, current L2I methods still exhibit suboptimal performance. Therefore, we propose InstanceAssemble, a novel architecture that incorporates layout conditions via instance-assembling attention, enabling position control with bounding boxes (bbox) and multimodal content control including texts and additional visual content. Our method achieves flexible adaption to existing DiT-based T2I models through light-weighted LoRA modules. Additionally, we propose a Layout-to-Image benchmark, Denselayout, a comprehensive benchmark for layout-to-image generation, containing 5k images with 90k instances in total. We further introduce Layout Grounding Score (LGS), an interpretable evaluation metric to more precisely assess the accuracy of L2I generation. Experiments demonstrate that our InstanceAssemble method achieves state-of-the-art performance under complex layout conditions, while exhibiting strong compatibility with diverse style LoRA modules. The code and pretrained models are publicly available at https://github.com/FireRedTeam/InstanceAssemble."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16691","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:54:21Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:54:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:35:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17569","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17569","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17569","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Quinn","name":"Quinn, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Buffoni","name":"Buffoni, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Gherardini","name":"Gherardini, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriele","familyName":"De Chiara","name":"De Chiara, Gabriele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Conditioning in Generative Quantum Denoising Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T11:01:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:07:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T14:25:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:07:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/2058-9565/ae8881","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum denoising diffusion models have recently emerged as a powerful framework for generative quantum machine learning. In this work, we extend these models by introducing a conditioning mechanism that enables the generation of quantum states drawn from multiple target distributions. By sharing parameters across distinct classes of quantum states, our approach avoids the need to train separate models for each distribution. We validate our method through numerical simulations that span single-qubit generation tasks, entangled state preparation, and many-body ground state generation. Across these tasks, conditioning significantly reduced the error of targeted state generation by more than an order of magnitude. Finally, we perform an ablation study to quantify the effect of key hyperparameters on the model performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Close to published version. 17 pages, 13 figures, 3 tables. Code, notebooks, and data at https://github.com/DanielQuinn53/CQDD"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17569","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:15:07Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:15:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T02:59:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17930","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17930","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17930","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Whitehill","name":"Whitehill, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transformer-Encoder Trees for Efficient Multilingual Machine Translation and Speech Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T15:52:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:43:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T14:57:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T01:11:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T19:40:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:08:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multilingual translation suffers from computational redundancy, especially when translating into multiple languages simultaneously. In addition, translation quality can suffer for low-resource languages. To address this, we introduce Transformer Encoder Tree (TET), a hierarchical, non-autoregressive encoder-only architecture trained with Connectionist Temporal Classification (CTC) for multilingual translation. TET shares intermediate representations among linguistically similar target languages, improving accuracy on low-resource languages while reducing computational redundancy and enabling the generation of all target languages in a single forward pass. TET eliminates the sequential bottleneck of autoregressive models and supports fully parallel decoding of all tokens across all target languages. Compared to a naive one-to-many multilingual design, TET reduces the total parameter count by 66% and lowers inference computation by 60%. In speech translation, combining TET with a non-autoregressive speech recognition backbone (Wav2Vec2) shows competitive translation quality compared to autoregressive systems while speeding up inference by approximately 7-14 times."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in the Findings of EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17930","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:23:37Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:23:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:00:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16625","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16625","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16625","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Guerra","name":"Guerra, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Chapuis","name":"Chapuis, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume","familyName":"Duc","name":"Duc, Guillaume","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavlo","familyName":"Mozharovskyi","name":"Mozharovskyi, Pavlo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Van-Tam","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Van-Tam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Supervised Learning of Graph Representations for Network Intrusion Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T11:02:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:26:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T16:30:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T01:15:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-17T08:36:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:32:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-05T12:05:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:33:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-20T22:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:38:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-16T16:57:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.52202/085713-3653","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detecting intrusions in network traffic is a challenging task, particularly under limited supervision and constantly evolving attack patterns. While recent works have leveraged graph neural networks for network intrusion detection, they often decouple representation learning from anomaly detection, limiting the utility of the embeddings for identifying attacks. We propose GraphIDS, a self-supervised intrusion detection model that unifies these two stages by learning local graph representations of normal communication patterns through a masked autoencoder. An inductive graph neural network embeds each flow with its local topological context to capture typical network behavior, while a Transformer-based encoder-decoder reconstructs these embeddings, implicitly learning global co-occurrence patterns via self-attention without requiring explicit positional information. During inference, flows with unusually high reconstruction errors are flagged as potential intrusions. This end-to-end framework ensures that embeddings are directly optimized for the downstream task, facilitating the recognition of malicious traffic. On diverse NetFlow benchmarks, GraphIDS achieves strong performance, reaching up to 99.98% PR-AUC and 99.61% macro F1-score."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16625","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:47Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:56:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonor","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Leonor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lea","familyName":"Hirlimann","name":"Hirlimann, Lea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucija Mihić","familyName":"Zidar","name":"Zidar, Lucija Mihić","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Wicke","name":"Wicke, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"Hofmann","name":"Hofmann, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hinrich","familyName":"Schütze","name":"Schütze, Hinrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SLAyiNG: A Diverse and Community-validated Dataset of Queer Slang"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T07:41:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:15:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T15:11:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:55:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Queer vernacular is rarely studied in NLP, despite advancements in resources and evaluation for other sociolects and informal language. Because of this, NLP systems often process queer language incorrectly, e.g., they misclassify it as hate speech or generate negative responses. To address this problem, we propose Slaying, the first real-world dataset of English queer slang. Slaying is community-validated, and includes over 500 queer slang terms that pertain to more than 20 queer subcommunities. We argue that queer language data resources have great potential in NLP -- e.g., as components of large pretraining corpora and as the basis for benchmarks -- and can improve queer users' experience of NLP systems. We leverage Slaying for two novel findings in support of this argument: (i) For a number of language models, we show that they are unbiased towards the queer community, but at the same time unable to process its language, i.e., absence of representation bias does not entail the absence of linguistic bias. (ii) Model performance on queer slang varies across queer subcommunities; it is generally worse for slang pertaining to African-American and Latine communities. These findings are relevant for both the queer NLP and the broader ML communities. Slaying is available to the public, and open to future revisions and extensions. Warning: This paper contains profane and potentially offensive language."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:12:15Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:12:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:22:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16749","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16749","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16749","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Bertiger","name":"Bertiger, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bobby","familyName":"Filar","name":"Filar, Bobby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aryan","familyName":"Luthra","name":"Luthra, Aryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Meschiari","name":"Meschiari, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aiden","familyName":"Mitchell","name":"Mitchell, Aiden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sam","familyName":"Scholten","name":"Scholten, Sam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vivek","familyName":"Sharath","name":"Sharath, Vivek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating LLM Generated Detection Rules in Cybersecurity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T17:21:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:06:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs are increasingly pervasive in the security environment, with limited measures of their effectiveness, which limits trust and usefulness to security practitioners. Here, we present an open-source evaluation framework and benchmark metrics for evaluating LLM-generated cybersecurity rules. The benchmark employs a holdout set-based methodology to measure the effectiveness of LLM-generated security rules in comparison to a human-generated corpus of rules. It provides three key metrics inspired by the way experts evaluate security rules, offering a realistic, multifaceted evaluation of the effectiveness of an LLM-based security rule generator. This methodology is illustrated using rules from Sublime Security's detection team and those written by Sublime Security's Automated Detection Engineer (ADE), with a thorough analysis of ADE's skills presented in the results section."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint of a paper accepted at the Conference on Applied Machine Learning in Information Security (CAMLIS 2025). 11 pages, 3 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16749","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:55:42Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:55:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-13T02:11:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shipeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dengfeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dengfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonglin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhonglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Describe-to-Score: A text-guided framework for image complexity assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T10:17:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:25:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T01:35:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:22:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurately assessing image complexity (IC) is essential for many vision tasks, yet existing approaches rely almost exclusively on visual features and therefore fail to capture the high-level semantics that humans often use when judging complexity. We introduce a multimodal perspective for IC modeling by integrating visual representations with caption-derived textual semantics. This integration enriches the representational space and provides complementary structural cues that are difficult to infer from vision alone. From an information theoretic and representation viewpoint, we offer an idealized analysis suggesting how semantic guidance can regularize the hypothesis space and support more stable generalization. We propose D2S (Describe-to-Score), a text-guided framework that uses caption-derived semantics only during training to regularize visual complexity modeling, while preserving a vision-only inference pipeline with no additional multimodal overhead at inference. Concretely, D2S transfers semantic structure into the visual branch through feature alignment and entropy distribution alignment, encouraging the visual encoder to internalize complexity-relevant semantic regularities. Experiments show that D2S achieves state-of-the-art performance on the IC9600 benchmark and remains competitive on no-reference image quality assessment (NR-IQA) tasks. Additional analyses further clarify the sample-imbalance issue in the small samples training setting and the distribution-shift limitations observed in cross-dataset transfer. Code is available at: https://github.com/xauat-liushipeng/D2S."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Pattern Recognition"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:24Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:12:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16462","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16462","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16462","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mina","familyName":"Arzaghi","name":"Arzaghi, Mina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza Dehghanpour","familyName":"Farashah","name":"Farashah, Alireza Dehghanpour","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Carichon","name":"Carichon, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean-François","familyName":"Plante","name":"Plante, Jean-François","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Golnoosh","familyName":"Farnadi","name":"Farnadi, Golnoosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Let's Unlearn Stereotypes Before Decision-Making: Assessing the Impact of Intrinsic Bias Mitigation on Downstream Fairness in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-19T22:59:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:11:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T19:07:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:04:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used in high-stakes decision-making systems, where biased predictions can reinforce social and economic disparities. Although prior work has examined intrinsic representational bias and unfair downstream behavior separately, it remains unclear whether mitigating intrinsic bias leads to fairer downstream outcomes. We introduce Fairness-Aware Concept Unlearning (FACU), a model-level mitigation method that adapts concept unlearning to fairness-oriented representation balancing. Unlike suppression-based approaches, FACU explicitly regularizes probability differences between stereotypical and anti-stereotypical associations while preserving predictive performance and language modeling quality. We evaluate FACU across three open-source LLMs, multiple intrinsic bias benchmarks, and three socio-economic classification datasets using both frozen LLM embeddings and LoRA-fine-tuned classifiers. FACU produces statistically significant reductions in intrinsic gender bias that are associated with downstream fairness improvements across most evaluated settings, datasets, models, and fairness metrics, without significantly degrading predictive performance. Combining FACU with extrinsic mitigation methods, particularly counterfactual data augmentation, yields further fairness improvements. These findings suggest that fairness-aware intrinsic mitigation can support fairer LLM-based decision-making and that bias mitigation should be addressed across both model development and downstream deployment stages."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16462","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:48:58Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:48:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:05:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmet Yavuz","familyName":"Uluslu","name":"Uluslu, Ahmet Yavuz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tannon","familyName":"Kew","name":"Kew, Tannon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tilia","familyName":"Ellendorff","name":"Ellendorff, Tilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerold","familyName":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, Gerold","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rico","familyName":"Sennrich","name":"Sennrich, Rico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Native Language Identification through Agentic Decomposition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T12:38:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:29:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T13:53:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:50:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.423","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) often achieve high performance in native language identification (NLI) benchmarks by leveraging superficial contextual clues such as names, locations, and cultural stereotypes, rather than the underlying linguistic patterns indicative of native language (L1) influence. To improve robustness, previous work has instructed LLMs to disregard such clues. In this work, we demonstrate that such a strategy is unreliable and model predictions can be easily altered by misleading hints. To address this problem, we introduce an agentic NLI pipeline inspired by forensic linguistics, where specialized agents accumulate and categorize diverse linguistic evidence before an independent final overall assessment. In this final assessment, a goal-aware coordinating agent synthesizes all evidence to make the NLI prediction. On two benchmark datasets, our approach significantly enhances NLI robustness against misleading contextual clues and performance consistency compared to standard prompting methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP* 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:53:45Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:53:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:14:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17394","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17394","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17394","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denis S.","familyName":"Grebenkov","name":"Grebenkov, Denis S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael J.","familyName":"Ward","name":"Ward, Michael J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Asymptotic Analysis of Some PDE and Steklov Eigenvalue Problems with Partially Reactive Patches in 3-D"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Spectral Theory (math.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T06:54:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:13:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T10:50:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:54:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-03T06:34:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-04T01:32:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider steady-state diffusion in a three-dimensional bounded domain with a smooth reflecting boundary that is partially covered by small partially reactive patches. By using the method of matched asymptotic expansions, we investigate the competition of these patches for a diffusing particle and the crucial role of surface reactions on these targets. After a brief overview of former contributions to this field, we first illustrate our approach by considering the classical problems of the mean first-reaction time (MFRT) and the splitting probability for partially reactive patches characterized by a Robin boundary condition. For a spherical domain, we derive a three-term asymptotic expansion for the MFRT and splitting probabilities in the small-patch limit. This expansion is valid for arbitrary reactivities, and also accounts for the effect of the spatial configuration of patches on the boundary. Secondly, we consider more intricate surface reactions modeled by mixed Steklov-Neumann or Steklov-Neumann-Dirichlet problems. We provide the first derivation of the asymptotic behavior of the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions for these spectral problems in the small-patch limit for a spherical domain. Extensions of these asymptotic results to arbitrary domains and their physical applications are discussed."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17394","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:10:57Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:10:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:35:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17192","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17192","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17192","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Glenn","familyName":"Matlin","name":"Matlin, Glenn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isaac","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Isaac","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiong","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Yixiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parv","familyName":"Mahajan","name":"Mahajan, Parv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Montoya","name":"Montoya, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Bard","name":"Bard, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stuart R.","familyName":"Topp","name":"Topp, Stuart R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony Wen-Ming","familyName":"Zang","name":"Zang, Anthony Wen-Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed Rehan","familyName":"Parwani","name":"Parwani, Mohammed Rehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soham","familyName":"Shetty","name":"Shetty, Soham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Riedl","name":"Riedl, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shall We Play a Game? Language Models for Open-ended Wargames"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T18:37:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:23:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T02:21:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:18:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-31T04:27:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-03T00:21:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based social simulations can make a generated transcript look like a single behavioral signal, but the model behind that transcript may be doing several different jobs: choosing what an actor says or does, deciding what happens after an action, or both. The difference matters especially in open-ended wargames, where models are prized for handling unusual actions and ambiguous consequences. We report a scoping review of 223 de-duplicated AI-in-wargames and strategic-simulation papers retrieved through May 1, 2026, describing each simulation by its model-control profile: whether the language model has open-ended control over player actions, adjudication, or both. Only 20 of 223 studies (~9%) give language models both roles. Before treating LM outputs as social simulations, researchers need to know how much creative control the model has over actions and consequences. For open-ended simulations, fidelity depends not only on whether agents behave plausibly, but also on whether language models can reliably act as adjudicators or world models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"49 pages (9-page body), 3 figures. Published at the Social Sim'26 Workshop at COLM 2026 (non-archival)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17192","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:06:12Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:06:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-03T02:08:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16780","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16780","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16780","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eason","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Eason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuangji","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chuangji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zimo","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Zimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jionghao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jionghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth R.","familyName":"Koedinger","name":"Koedinger, Kenneth R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Comparing RAG and GraphRAG for Page-Level Retrieval Question Answering on a Math Textbook"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T19:06:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:38:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T05:24:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:32:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T14:37:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-29T00:55:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) show promise as educational aids but often lack alignment with specific course materials. We investigate Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and GraphRAG for page-level question answering on an undergraduate mathematics textbook. Using a curated dataset of 477 question-answer pairs, each tied to a specific textbook page, we compare five embedding-based RAG models, a BM25 baseline, and GraphRAG across two metrics: retrieval accuracy (whether the correct page is retrieved) and answer quality (F1 score). Our results show that embedding-based RAG outperforms GraphRAG for page-level retrieval, with voyage-3-large achieving 99.4% accuracy at top-10 (bootstrap 95% CI for top-1: [.644, .728]). BM25 proves a strong baseline, outperforming several embedding models. Error analysis reveals that 63.3% of top-1 failures retrieve same-chapter content, suggesting pedagogical relevance even in failure cases. GraphRAG retrieves excessive context (~47K tokens vs. ~3.7K for RAG), reducing generation quality. We further replicate key experiments using an open-source local LLM (Qwen3.5-35B-A3B), finding that RAG benefits are proportionally larger for weaker models (+39% vs. +16% relative F1 improvement), an important result for cost-sensitive educational deployments. These findings inform the design of AI tutoring systems that reference specific textbook pages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16780","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:56:25Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:56:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-29T02:27:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":529,"totalPages":22,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026sort=registered\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-22T16%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202025-09-23T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D%20AND%20(titles.title%3A(%22language%20model%22%20OR%20%22language%20models%22%20OR%20LLM%20OR%20LLMs%20OR%20transformer%20OR%20transformers%20OR%20%22foundation%20model%22%20OR%20%22foundation%20models%22%20OR%20agent%20OR%20agents%20OR%20%22vision%20language%22%20OR%20%22vision-language%22%20OR%20multimodal%20OR%20diffusion%20OR%20%22world%20model%22%20OR%20%22world%20models%22%20OR%20%22mixture%20of%20experts%22%20OR%20%22mixture-of-experts%22)%20OR%20descriptions.description%3A(%22language%20model%22%20OR%20%22language%20models%22%20OR%20LLM%20OR%20LLMs%20OR%20transformer%20OR%20transformers%20OR%20%22foundation%20model%22%20OR%20%22foundation%20models%22%20OR%20agent%20OR%20agents%20OR%20%22vision%20language%22%20OR%20%22vision-language%22%20OR%20multimodal%20OR%20diffusion%20OR%20%